-- Q2403: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, a CPA should not recommend a tax return position unless there is at least a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits. Per IRC Sec. 6694, this aligns with the substantial authority standard for undisclosed positions and the reasonable basis standard for disclosed positions.
Wrong (B): The CPA need not believe the position will definitely be sustained. SSTS No. 1 requires only a realistic possibility, not certainty.
Wrong (C): A CPA cannot recommend a position based solely on client wishes without adequate legal support and authority.
Wrong (D): IRS pre-approval is not required. The CPA must independently evaluate the position against applicable tax authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2403;

-- Q2404: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, the realistic possibility standard requires approximately a one-in-three (33%) likelihood that the position will be sustained on its merits if challenged. This is the minimum confidence level before a CPA may recommend a position without disclosure.
Wrong (A): A greater than 50% chance describes the more-likely-than-not standard under IRC Sec. 6662(d)(2)(B), which is a higher threshold than realistic possibility.
Wrong (C): SSTS No. 1 does not require specific published IRS guidance authorizing each position taken on a return.
Wrong (D): A private letter ruling is not required. The realistic possibility standard is met through analysis of applicable tax authority, not IRS pre-approval.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2404;

-- Q2405: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Circular 230 Section 10.21, when a practitioner discovers a client''s noncompliance with tax obligations, the practitioner must promptly advise the client of the noncompliance, its consequences, and the corrective steps available. The obligation runs to the client, not to the government.
Wrong (A): Circular 230 does not require the practitioner to report the client to the IRS Criminal Investigation Division. The client-practitioner relationship is maintained.
Wrong (B): Termination is not automatically required. The practitioner must advise the client and may continue the engagement if the client takes corrective action.
Wrong (C): The practitioner cannot unilaterally file corrective returns on behalf of the client. The decision to amend rests with the client.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2405;

