-- Q2407: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AICPA SSTS No. 4 (Use of Estimates), a CPA may use estimates on a tax return when it is impracticable to obtain exact data and the CPA determines the estimates are reasonable based on known facts and circumstances. Estimates should not be presented as exact figures.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 4 does not require specific IRS guidance authorizing estimates for a particular item. The standard permits estimates whenever exact data is unavailable.
Wrong (C): There is no $500 materiality threshold in SSTS No. 4. The standard applies based on reasonableness, not a fixed dollar amount.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 4 explicitly permits the use of reasonable estimates. It does not require exact figures for every item on the return.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2407;

-- Q2409: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SSTS No. 2 (Answers to Questions on Returns), a CPA should make a reasonable effort to obtain information but may leave a question unanswered when the information is not readily available and the answer is not significant in determining the correct tax liability.
Wrong (A): A CPA cannot leave any question unanswered without limitation. SSTS No. 2 requires the CPA to evaluate whether the omission may cause the return to be deemed incomplete.
Wrong (C): A written client directive is not sufficient by itself. The CPA must independently assess the significance of the omitted answer.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 2 does not require every question to be answered. It permits omissions when the information is unavailable and insignificant to the tax liability.$EXPL$
