-- Q2411: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA SSTS No. 5, a CPA may recommend a position contrary to a prior IRS administrative settlement when the facts and circumstances have changed or new legal authority supports the different position. The standard requires an objective basis to justify the departure.
Wrong (A): The mere passage of time does not justify departing from a prior settlement. SSTS No. 5 requires changed facts or new authority, not a waiting period.
Wrong (B): Client insistence alone is insufficient. The CPA must have an independent objective basis such as changed facts or new legal authority.
Wrong (D): The likelihood of a future audit is irrelevant. SSTS No. 5 requires substantive justification, not an assessment of audit risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2411;

-- Q2412: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, a CPA should not recommend a tax return position unless the CPA has a good-faith belief that the position has a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits if challenged. This is generally interpreted as approximately a one-in-three likelihood.
Wrong (A): Certainty is not required. SSTS No. 1 sets the threshold at realistic possibility, which is well below absolute certainty.
Wrong (B): A private letter ruling is not required before recommending any position. The CPA evaluates the position against existing tax authority.
Wrong (C): A 75% probability exceeds the SSTS No. 1 standard. The realistic possibility threshold is approximately 33%, not 75%.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2412;

-- Q2414: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA SSTS No. 6 (Knowledge of Error), when a CPA becomes aware of a possible error on a previously filed return, regardless of who prepared it, the CPA should promptly inform the client and recommend corrective measures such as filing an amended return.
Wrong (A): The CPA cannot ignore the error simply because another firm prepared the prior return. SSTS No. 6 applies regardless of who was the original preparer.
Wrong (B): The CPA should not contact the prior firm without the client''s consent. The obligation runs to the client, not the prior preparer.
Wrong (D): The CPA has no obligation or authority to report errors to the IRS whistleblower office. The duty is to advise the client of corrective action.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2414;

-- Q2416: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct, CPAs performing tax services must maintain integrity and objectivity. Unlike attest engagements, tax engagements do not require independence from the client, but the CPA must not subordinate professional judgment to client wishes.
Wrong (A): Independence identical to attest engagements is not required for tax services. The AICPA independence rules apply primarily to audit and review engagements.
Wrong (B): CPAs have ethical obligations beyond IRS requirements, including the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct and applicable SSTS standards.
Wrong (C): Client loyalty does not override all other professional obligations. The CPA must balance client advocacy with integrity and objectivity.$EXPL$
