-- Q2419: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AICPA SSTS No. 3, when a discrepancy exists between a third-party information return and the client''s claim, the CPA must make reasonable inquiries, request supporting documentation, and exercise professional judgment in determining the correct amount to report.
Wrong (A): Blindly accepting the 1099 amount without inquiry ignores the CPA''s duty to exercise professional judgment when information suggests a discrepancy.
Wrong (B): Splitting the difference has no basis in professional standards. SSTS No. 3 requires investigation, not arbitrary compromise.
Wrong (C): Accepting the client''s oral statement alone is insufficient. The CPA must request supporting documentation and evaluate the evidence before determining the correct amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2419;

-- Q2421: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct, independence rules apply primarily to attest (audit and review) engagements. A CPA may perform tax preparation and bookkeeping services for the same client without violating independence rules, since these are non-attest services.
Wrong (A): Performing both tax and bookkeeping services does not violate independence rules. An independence issue arises only if the CPA also performs attest services for the same client.
Wrong (B): No waiver from the state board is required for providing multiple non-attest services to the same client.
Wrong (D): No IRS disclosure is required for performing dual non-attest services. The IRS does not regulate AICPA independence standards.$EXPL$
