-- Q2432: Tax Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 7803(e) (as amended by the Taxpayer First Act), after receiving a 30-day letter, a taxpayer may request a conference with the Independent Office of Appeals for an independent administrative review. For deficiencies over $25,000, a formal written protest is required.
Wrong (B): The U.S. Supreme Court does not hear tax disputes directly. Tax litigation begins in the Tax Court, District Court, or Court of Federal Claims.
Wrong (C): A written protest goes to the Appeals Office, not back to the examining agent. The examiner''s role ends once the 30-day letter is issued.
Wrong (D): Congressional representatives have no formal role in the IRS appeals process. The administrative appeal is handled through the Independent Office of Appeals.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2432;

-- Q2433: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under 28 U.S.C. Sec. 1346(a)(1), the U.S. District Court has jurisdiction over federal tax refund suits and is the only court that provides the right to a jury trial in tax disputes. The taxpayer must first pay the assessed tax and file a refund claim under IRC Sec. 7422(a).
Wrong (A): The U.S. Tax Court adjudicates deficiency cases before judges without juries. It does not offer jury trials.
Wrong (B): The U.S. Court of Federal Claims under 28 U.S.C. Sec. 1491 also hears tax refund suits but conducts bench trials only, with no jury right.
Wrong (D): The IRS Appeals Office is an administrative body, not a court. It cannot conduct trials or provide jury proceedings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2433;

-- Q2434: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6651(a)(1), the failure-to-file penalty is 5% of the unpaid tax for each month or part of a month the return is late, up to a maximum of 25%. This penalty is separate from and in addition to the failure-to-pay penalty.
Wrong (B): The 0.5% per month rate is the failure-to-pay penalty under Section 6651(a)(2), not the failure-to-file penalty.
Wrong (C): The failure-to-file penalty is percentage-based, not a flat amount. It is calculated as a percentage of the unpaid tax for each month of delinquency.
Wrong (D): The penalty is 5% per month up to 25%, not a flat 10% of total liability. The calculation is based on unpaid tax, not total tax liability for the year.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2434;

-- Q2435: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 6651(a)(2), the failure-to-pay penalty is 0.5% of the unpaid tax for each month or part of a month the tax remains unpaid, up to a maximum of 25%. The rate increases to 1% per month after an IRS notice of intent to levy if the tax remains unpaid for 10 days.
Wrong (A): The 5% per month rate is the failure-to-file penalty under Section 6651(a)(1), not the failure-to-pay penalty.
Wrong (B): The failure-to-pay penalty is a monthly percentage, not a flat 10% charge on the unpaid balance.
Wrong (C): Filing on time avoids the failure-to-file penalty but does not eliminate the failure-to-pay penalty when the full tax is not remitted.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2435;

-- Q2436: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6662, the accuracy-related penalty is 20% of the underpayment attributable to negligence, disregard of rules, or substantial understatement. The penalty can be avoided if the taxpayer demonstrates reasonable cause and good faith under Section 6664(c).
Wrong (A): The 5% rate does not correspond to any accuracy-related penalty under Section 6662. It is the monthly failure-to-file penalty rate under Section 6651.
Wrong (B): There is no 10% accuracy-related penalty rate. Section 6662 imposes a flat 20% penalty on the applicable underpayment.
Wrong (D): The 75% rate applies to civil fraud under Section 6663, not negligence. Fraud requires clear and convincing evidence of intent to evade tax.$EXPL$
